1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
ChipQC: organ-on-a-chip image review.
A working local tool and reproducible study.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
3,072 brightfield frames; six cell types;
59 acquisition-date prefixes.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Does a model handle unseen imaging dates?
Scores estimate the experts' good/bad label.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,081
Actual running application. This checkpoint excludes the acquisition dates used in
these examples.

5
00:00:22,081 --> 00:00:23,840
Upload example 230320_47; source expert label: good.

6
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:31,110
Predicted good probability: 92.3%. The example illustrates the interface, not
overall accuracy.

7
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:32,851
Upload example 230524_24; source expert label: bad.

8
00:00:32,851 --> 00:00:40,124
Predicted good probability: 7.7%. The example illustrates the interface, not overall
accuracy.

9
00:00:40,124 --> 00:00:41,884
Upload example 230524_73; source expert label: good.

10
00:00:41,884 --> 00:00:48,936
An uncertain estimate stays in the manual-review band. Scientist review is
recommended.

11
00:00:48,936 --> 00:00:55,430
Focus and appearance measurements support review. They do not establish biological
function or safety.

12
00:00:55,430 --> 00:01:09,430
Five candidates: compare random image folds
with folds excluding entire acquisition dates.

13
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:17,430
Selected model: MobileNet + logistic regression.
AUC: 0.748 with dates held out; 0.816 on random folds.

14
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:26,430
Date-bootstrap 95% interval: 0.694 to 0.797.
New labs and biological function remain untested.

15
00:01:26,430 --> 00:01:34,430
Install pinned dependencies, then fetch images
and compute features and MobileNet embeddings.

16
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:43,430
Run the evaluation and follow the README
to start the held-out demonstration checkpoint.

17
00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:49,430
Images: Movčana et al., Zenodo 2023.
DOI 10.5281/zenodo.10203721; CC BY 4.0.

18
00:01:49,430 --> 00:01:55,416
Original code: MIT. A research review aid.
Scientists retain the decision.
